[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Event Sourcing and Append-Only Logs in Database Design"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction to Event Sourcing"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"Event Sourcing is an architectural pattern where state changes are stored as a sequence of immutable events rather than overwriting current state data. Instead of updating a single row with the latest values, every business action is captured as a discrete event."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"The Role of Append-Only Logs"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"At the core of an event-sourced system is the append-only log. Writing data exclusively through appends maximizes write throughput because it eliminates disk seeks associated with random updates. The log acts as the single source of truth."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Immutability: Past events cannot be altered or deleted, ensuring a complete audit trail."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"High Concurrency: Append-only structures minimize lock contention during write operations."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Time-Travel Queries: Systems can reconstruct state at any exact timestamp by replaying events."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Data Model Implementation"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"An event store table typically requires minimal columns. Below is a standard schema definition designed for high-performance append operations:"},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"CREATE TABLE event_log (
    event_id UUID PRIMARY KEY,
    aggregate_id UUID NOT NULL,
    sequence_number BIGINT NOT NULL,
    event_type VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
    event_payload JSONB NOT NULL,
    created_at TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
    UNIQUE(aggregate_id, sequence_number)
);"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Rebuilding State via Folding"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"To read the current state of an aggregate, the application queries all events associated with the specific aggregate ID and folds them sequentially. In languages like JavaScript or Python, this is typically handled via a reduce function."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"function reconstructState(events) {
    return events.reduce((state, event) => {
        switch (event.event_type) {
            case 'AccountCreated':
                return { ...state, balance: event.payload.initialBalance, active: true };
            case 'FundsDeposited':
                return { ...state, balance: state.balance + event.payload.amount };
            default:
                return state;
        }
    }, {});
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Performance Optimization: Snapshots"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Replaying thousands of historical events for a single read operation introduces latency. To mitigate this, systems periodically write state 'snapshots'. When rebuilding state, the application loads the latest snapshot and replays only subsequent events."}]
